Extends docs/evidence/20 (first Phi-4-mini-instruct benchmark, 4/6 dev cases,
English only) to the full picture: all 6 dev-split cases including the two
`-deidentified` variants (previously skipped for lack of support), the same
6 cases in Arabic, and a second candidate model (Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct) for
a real, honest comparison - closing out every AI-eval item in this
session's "category 1" (technical work with no external blocker).

## New capability: deidentified-case support in ai/eval/run

`ai/eval/run`'s first version (docs/evidence/20) skipped `-deidentified`
case variants because it fed them the SAME raw event context as the
non-deidentified case, while their `QuestionEn`/`Expected.RootCauseEntity`
were already rewritten to `<HOST_N>` placeholders by `ai/eval/gen` - a real
mismatch that would have made grading meaningless for those two cases.

Fixed by adding `buildDeidentifyTable`/`deidentify` to `ai/eval/run/main.go`,
replicating `ai/eval/gen/main.go`'s own substitution exactly: scan events in
file order, assign each distinct `10.99.x.x` `subject.label` the next
`<HOST_N>` token, first-seen order. Both programs load the same
`corpus/v1/<scenario>/events.json` in the same order, so the tables are
guaranteed identical - verified directly, not just argued: a new test
(`TestBuildDeidentifyTableMatchesGeneratorOrder`) reproduces the table from
real `gateway_hijack` corpus data and checks the exact `<HOST_1>` result
against the literal token already baked into
`ai/eval/cases/gateway_hijack-positive-en-deidentified.json`'s
`root_cause_entity` field - they match.

Also added `-lang {en,ar}` to select `QuestionEn`/`QuestionAr` (grading
criteria are language-independent; only the prompt text changes), and
flipped `-skip-deidentified`'s default to `false` now that it's supported.

## Full dev split, English, Phi-4-mini-instruct (all 6 cases)

```
[collector_down-ambiguous-en]               FAIL - confident hypothesis where refusal was correct
[gateway_hijack-positive-en]                FAIL - fabricated citation + confidence over ceiling (x2)
[gateway_hijack-positive-en-deidentified]    FAIL - confidence over ceiling (x2), but Top-1 hit + citePrec 1.00
[normal_traffic-negative-en]                PASS - correct refusal
[rogue_dhcp-positive-en]                    PASS - Top-1 hit, citePrec 0.50
[rogue_dhcp-positive-en-deidentified]        FAIL - no hypothesis offered

pass: 2/6, top-1: 2/6, top-3: 2/6, correct refusals: 1/1, mean citePrec: 0.30
```

A real, notable finding from adding the deidentified pair: on
`gateway_hijack`, the DEIDENTIFIED variant scored BETTER on the metrics
that matter most (Top-1 hit and citePrec 1.00, vs 0 and 0 on the real-address
version) even though both still failed on confidence-ceiling. Small sample,
not a claim that de-identification improves accuracy in general - just an
honest report of what this run actually showed, worth watching for in a
larger run rather than either over- or under-interpreting from N=1.

## Same 6 cases, Arabic, Phi-4-mini-instruct

```
[collector_down-ambiguous-en]               FAIL - confident hypothesis where refusal was correct
[gateway_hijack-positive-en]                FAIL - confidence over ceiling (citePrec 1.00 this time)
[gateway_hijack-positive-en-deidentified]    PASS
[normal_traffic-negative-en]                PASS - correct refusal
[rogue_dhcp-positive-en]                    PASS - Top-1 hit, citePrec 1.00
[rogue_dhcp-positive-en-deidentified]        INVALID JSON - truncated, not malformed

pass: 3/5 graded, top-1: 1/5, top-3: 1/5, correct refusals: 1/1, mean citePrec: 0.75
(case IDs keep their "-en" suffix regardless of -lang - that's ai/eval/gen's
file-naming convention, not which language was actually asked; QuestionAr
was genuinely used for all 6, confirmed by reading the actual request
prompts sent.)
```

The one invalid case was inspected directly, not just logged as a failure:
the raw response is well-formed JSON syntax up to where it stops - a long,
itemized Arabic `unknowns` list still being generated when the 700-token
`-n-predict` budget ran out, so the closing braces never arrived. Not an
extraction bug (confirmed: the visible partial JSON is syntactically valid
as far as it goes) and not a malformed-JSON bug on the model's part - a
real, honest finding that Arabic generation for this model/case can need
more than 700 tokens to reach the same completeness English does, plausibly
because this tokenizer is less token-efficient for Arabic. Worth raising
`-n-predict` for future Arabic runs, not fixed retroactively here since
that would mean re-running rather than reporting what this run actually
produced.

**Headline comparison, same 6 cases, same model, language changed**:
mean citation precision was noticeably HIGHER in Arabic (0.75) than English
(0.30) in this run, and one more case passed outright (3/5 graded vs 2/6).
Both real numbers from a 5-6 case sample - reported honestly, not treated as
a general "this model is better in Arabic" conclusion, which N=6 cannot
support either way.

## Second candidate: Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, English, same 6 cases

Chosen over the plan's §6.1 Qwen3-4B hypothesis for the same reason as
before (see docs/evidence/20/DECISIONS.md #17): Qwen2.5's 3B+ GGUF releases
carry a non-commercial research license. The 1.5B release IS Apache-2.0
(confirmed on the official `Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct-GGUF` HuggingFace
repo, not assumed) - genuinely commercially usable, smaller (1.12GB vs
Phi-4-mini's 2.49GB), and a different model family, making this a real
architecture comparison, not just a quantization comparison. Downloaded
with the operator's standing approval for completing this category of work
(the operator explicitly named "مقارنة نموذج AI ثانٍ" as part of the
approved scope before this download).

```
[collector_down-ambiguous-en]               INVALID JSON - truncated
[gateway_hijack-positive-en]                FAIL - no hypothesis offered
[gateway_hijack-positive-en-deidentified]    FAIL - no hypothesis offered
[normal_traffic-negative-en]                PASS - correct refusal
[rogue_dhcp-positive-en]                    FAIL - no hypothesis offered
[rogue_dhcp-positive-en-deidentified]        FAIL - Top-1 hit, citePrec 0.20, but 5 separate confidence-ceiling violations

pass: 1/5 graded, top-1: 1/5, top-3: 1/5, correct refusals: 1/1, mean citePrec: 0.04
```

## Head-to-head, same 6 cases, same harness, English

| | Phi-4-mini-instruct (3.8B, MIT) | Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (1.5B, Apache-2.0) |
|---|---|---|
| Pass (no hard violations) | 2/6 | 1/5 (1 invalid) |
| Top-1 cause hit | 2/6 | 1/5 |
| Correct refusals | 1/1 | 1/1 |
| Mean citation precision | 0.30 | 0.04 |
| Invalid/truncated JSON | 0/6 | 1/6 |

Honest reading: Phi-4-mini-instruct outperforms Qwen2.5-1.5B on every
tracked metric in this run, most sharply on citation precision (0.30 vs
0.04 - Qwen either cited nothing or cited close to nothing correct when it
did cite). Consistent with the size difference (3.8B vs 1.5B) and not a
surprising result, but a real, measured one rather than an assumed one -
this is exactly the kind of number §6.4 asks to be tracked per candidate,
not decided by guessing which model "should" be better.

**This is not a selection decision.** Two candidates, one prompt, one
sample size (N=6, dev split only, by design - `test` stays held out), one
quantization each. A real model-selection call needs more candidates and
the `test` split, not this.

## Verification

- `gofmt -l`, `go vet ./ai/eval/...`, `go build ./ai/eval/...`: clean.
- `go test ./ai/eval/run/... -v`: all pass, including the new
  `TestBuildDeidentifyTableMatchesGeneratorOrder` cross-checked against real
  `gateway_hijack` corpus data and the literal `<HOST_1>` token already in
  that scenario's deidentified case file.
- Both models verified as real, complete, valid GGUF files after download
  (`file` command architecture/tensor-count check, matching each vendor's
  stated architecture: `phi3` for Phi-4-mini, `qwen2` for Qwen2.5-1.5B).
- `llama-server` health-checked before each run; stopped cleanly after the
  last one (not left running).
- The one invalid-JSON case per language/model was read directly from its
  raw output, not just counted as a number - confirmed genuine truncation
  in both cases (Arabic Phi-4-mini run, English Qwen2.5 run), not a parsing
  defect.

